  "Number": 11,
  "Question": "Given \\(2^x=8^{y+1}\\) and \\(9^y=3^{x-9},\\) find the value of \\(x+y.\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(18\\)",
    "B": "\\(21\\)",
    "C": "\\(24\\)",
    "D": "\\(27\\)",
    "E": "\\(30\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The numbers \\(8\\) and \\(9\\) are powers of \\(2\\) and \\(3\\), respectively. Rewriting them makes the exponents directly comparable:\n\\[\n2^x=2^{3(y+1)},\n\\qquad 3^{2y}=3^{x-9}.\n\\]\nFor a fixed base greater than \\(1\\), distinct real exponents give distinct powers. Therefore these equations are equivalent to\n\\[\nx=3y+3,\n\\qquad 2y=x-9.\n\\]\nThe second equation gives \\(x=2y+9\\). Substituting this into the first eliminates \\(x\\):\n\\[\n2y+9=3y+3,\n\\qquad y=6.\n\\]\nThen \\(x=2(6)+9=21\\), so\n\\[\nx+y=21+6=\\boxed{27}.\n\\]\nThe answer is D.",
